## 2.10 Research Gap

Existing studies have demonstrated that adversarial attacks can reduce the performance of machine-learning models and that adversarial training can improve model robustness. However, an important limitation remains: a defense that performs well against the adversarial attack used during training may not provide the same level of protection against different or previously unseen attacks.

Previous research often evaluates adversarial robustness using the same attack employed during defensive training or includes several adversarial attacks during the training process. Although these approaches are useful for measuring robustness, they provide limited evidence about whether protection learned from a **single attack** can transfer to different adversarial techniques.

Therefore, the specific research gap addressed in this study is:

> **There is limited empirical evidence on whether FGSM-based adversarial training in an ML-based intrusion detection system can generalize its robustness to unseen adversarial attacks such as JSMA and DeepFool.**

To address this gap, the proposed research uses CIC-IDS2017 to train a Logistic Regression-based intrusion detection model. FGSM is used for adversarial training and represents the **seen attack**. The defended model is then tested against JSMA and DeepFool as **unseen attacks**, together with FGSM for comparison.

The performance of the model before and after adversarial training will be compared using Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and Attack Success Rate (ASR). This comparison will help determine whether the defense is mainly effective against FGSM or whether its robustness can generalize to different adversarial attack techniques.

---

## 2.11 Chapter Summary

This chapter reviewed the literature related to Adversarial Machine Learning, ML-based intrusion detection, FGSM, JSMA, DeepFool, and adversarial training. Existing research demonstrates that ML-based security models can be vulnerable to adversarial manipulation and that adversarial training can improve their robustness.

However, FGSM, JSMA, and DeepFool use different methods to generate adversarial examples. Therefore, successful defense against one attack does not necessarily guarantee protection against another. This creates the central research gap concerning cross-attack generalization.

The proposed study addresses this gap by training the defense using FGSM and subsequently testing the defended Logistic Regression model against FGSM, JSMA, and DeepFool. The comparison between the seen FGSM attack and unseen JSMA and DeepFool attacks will determine whether FGSM-based adversarial training produces attack-specific protection or broader adversarial robustness.

